## Claim
Text: "Illegal drug, border crossings, and human smuggling activities have decreased in areas where barriers are deployed in the United States."
Claim author: Chase Jennings
Claim date: October 29, 2020
Claim origin: https://twitter.com/spoxdhs/status/1322031666739748864?s=21

## Initial Q&A
### What specific types of physical barriers had the U.S. government deployed along the U.S.–Mexico border as of October 2020?
Answer: Based on the search result, as of October 2020, the U.S. government had deployed several types of physical barriers along the U.S.–Mexico border, including: pedestrian fencing made of bollard, steel mesh, and chain link; vehicle fencing using bollard and Normandy-style designs; and levee wall systems consisting of "steel bollard on top of a concrete wall." The source notes that "CBP uses various materials for pedestrian fencing, including bollard, steel mesh, and chain link, and employs bollard and Normandy-style fencing for vehicle barriers," and that approximately 300 miles of primary fencing was designed to prevent vehicle entry while approximately 350 additional miles was designed to block people on foot.

Source URL: https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/20190827_R45888_4cf22df8c41ec70f5f3ff9e96a491dc15b800056.html

### Which specific U.S. border sectors had barriers fully constructed and operational as of October 2020?
Answer: Based on the search result, the **San Diego Sector** is specifically identified as having barriers fully constructed and operational as of October 2020, with "Construction workers recently completed 14 miles of new border wall system located within the San Diego area." The article also notes that construction was just beginning in the Rio Grande Valley Sector at the end of October 2020, and mentions Tucson Sector only in the context of a previous assignment, not as having newly completed barriers at that time.

Source URL: https://www.cbp.gov/frontline/wall-construction-continues-part-holistic-approach-stop-illegal-immigration

### What official U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) apprehension statistics compare illegal border crossings in barrier sectors versus non-barrier sectors during the period referenced in the claim?
Answer: According to the DHS press release from October 29, 2020, CBP reported several apprehension statistics comparing periods before and after border wall system deployment (rather than direct barrier vs. non-barrier sector comparisons). Key figures include: "Illegal entries in areas with new border wall system plummeted over 87% in FY 20 compared to FY 19"; in RGV Zone 1, "CBP has seen 79% decrease in apprehensions in this area (Zone 1) since the completion of border wall system"; in El Paso Sector's Santa Teresa Zones 14 and 15, "apprehensions have decreased by 60% and 81% respectively when comparing the last half of fiscal year FY 20 to the first half of FY 20"; and in El Paso Station Zones 20–23, "a reduction in apprehensions of 70% during the same timeframe." The release does not provide a direct side-by-side comparison of apprehension totals between barrier sectors and non-barrier sectors during this period.

Source URL: https://www.dhs.gov/news/2020/10/29/border-wall-system-deployed-effective-and-disrupting-criminals-and-smugglers

### Has the U.S. Department of Homeland Security or any independent organization published data showing that human smuggling incidents decreased in U.S. border sectors where barriers were deployed?
Answer: The search result is a 1995 GAO report (an independent organization) that does provide data showing reduced illegal entries in specific border sectors where barriers were deployed. For example, it states that "El Paso sector's illegal alien apprehensions in fiscal year 1994 were down 72 percent compared to fiscal year 1993" following the implementation of barrier/prevention strategies, and San Diego sector apprehensions "were down 20 percent in fiscal year 1994 compared to 1992." However, the report also notes that these decreases were offset by increases in other sectors (e.g., Tucson sector apprehensions rose about 50 percent), indicating displacement rather than an overall reduction. Additionally, this report predates the creation of DHS (2002) and addresses illegal immigration generally rather than "human smuggling" as a distinct category, so it does not directly support the specific 2020 DHS claim being fact-checked.

Source URL: https://www.gao.gov/assets/ggd-95-30.pdf

### Do independent analyses of CBP data, such as those from the Government Accountability Office (GAO) or academic researchers, confirm that border barriers caused measurable decreases in illegal crossings and drug smuggling?
Answer: The 1995 GAO report provides mixed evidence: it documents measurable decreases in illegal crossings in specific sectors where barriers were deployed (San Diego apprehensions dropped 20% and El Paso apprehensions dropped 72%), and cites two independent studies—a Center for Immigration Studies analysis and a University of Texas at Austin study—that confirmed Operation Hold-the-Line "significantly reduced illegal crossings." However, the GAO also found that "some drug smuggling and illegal immigration seems to have been rerouted from these two sectors to other southwest border areas," with Tucson sector apprehensions increasing about 50% and other sectors seeing a 17% rise, and concluded that "it is too early to tell what impact it will eventually have on drug smuggling and illegal immigration along the southwest border."

Source URL: https://www.gao.gov/assets/ggd-95-30.pdf

### How do apprehension numbers in U.S. border sectors with barriers compare before and after barrier construction, accounting for changes in migration flows, seasonal patterns, and policy shifts such as the Migrant Protection Protocols (MPP)?
Answer: The search result provides sector-by-sector apprehension data showing significant decreases after barrier construction: San Diego dropped from 565,000 apprehensions in 1992 to 151,000 by 2000 after fencing was built; El Centro fell from 238,000 (2000) to 32,000 (FY 2010); Yuma dropped from 110,000 (2000) to 7,100 (FY 2010); and Tucson saw a decrease of more than 400,000 between 2000 and 2010. However, the source notes these decreases "reflect a dramatic downturn in attempted entries" driven by both enforcement efforts and "the severe economic downturn," and it does not address seasonal patterns or the Migrant Protection Protocols (MPP), which was implemented in January 2019—after the data presented here.

Source URL: https://www.americanprogress.org/press/release-u-s-mexico-border-is-safer-than-ever/

### Did the COVID-19 pandemic and related Title 42 expulsions (implemented March 2020) coincide with the time frame referenced in the claim, and could these factors explain any observed decreases in border crossings independent of barrier construction?
Answer: Yes, the time frames coincide. Title 42 expulsions were implemented on March 21, 2020, in response to COVID-19, and the claim was made on October 29, 2020—approximately seven months later. According to the source, under this order, "CBP is prohibiting the entry of certain persons who potentially pose a health risk" and "persons subject to the order will not be held in congregate areas for processing and instead will immediately be expelled to their country of last transit." Because these expulsions were "tracked separately from immigration enforcement actions," any observed decreases in border crossings during this period could plausibly be attributed to Title 42 public health measures rather than to barrier construction.

Source URL: https://www.cbp.gov/newsroom/stats/cbp-enforcement-statistics/title-8-and-title-42-statistics-fy2021

### Has the DHS spokesperson or the Department of Homeland Security publicly clarified or provided documentation substantiating the specific magnitudes of decrease in illegal drug trafficking, border crossings, and human smuggling attributed to barriers?
Answer: Based on the search result, there is no evidence that DHS or its spokesperson publicly clarified or provided documentation substantiating specific magnitudes of decrease in illegal drug trafficking, border crossings, and human smuggling attributed to barriers. In fact, the CRS report indicates the opposite: GAO reported in 2017 that "CBP has not developed metrics that systematically use these, among other data it collects, to assess the contributions of border fencing to its mission," and GAO also reported in 2016 that "the border barriers' contributions to CBP goals were not" systematically assessed. The CRS report itself poses the open question: "Are existing barriers and completed improvements having measurable impacts on illegal entries into the U.S. and smuggling of contraband? How are CBP and other stakeholders making their assessments?"—suggesting that such substantiation was lacking.

Source URL: https://sgp.fas.org/crs/homesec/R45888.pdf

## Final Judgement
## Step 1: Summary of Key Findings

The fact-check reveals that the claim originates from a DHS press release/tweet on October 29, 2020. The DHS data does show significant decreases in apprehensions in specific border sectors where barriers were deployed (e.g., 87% in FY20 vs FY19 in areas with new border wall; 79% in RGV Zone 1; 60-81% in El Paso zones). However, several critical context points are missing from the claim: (1) Title 42 expulsions were implemented on March 21, 2020—seven months before the claim—creating a major confounding factor that would itself cause decreases in border crossings independent of barriers; (2) the 1995 GAO report showed that decreases in barrier sectors were offset by increases in non-barrier sectors (displacement effect); (3) the claim extends to "illegal drug" and "human smuggling" activities, but the evidence primarily documents apprehension/crossing decreases, not drug or human smuggling specifically; and (4) GAO reported in 2017 that CBP had not developed systematic metrics to assess border fencing's contribution to its mission.

## Step 2: Remaining Gaps

The fact-check has sufficient information to make a determination. The key question is not whether decreases occurred in barrier areas, but whether the claim fairly represents reality given the exclusion of important context.

## Step 3: Conclusion

The claim is technically partially supported by DHS data showing decreases in apprehensions in specific barrier sectors during the relevant timeframe. However, it cherry-picks the data by omitting the critical context that Title 42 public health expulsions were simultaneously implemented and would themselves cause dramatic decreases in border crossings. Additionally, the claim extends to "illegal drug" and "human smuggling" activities that the evidence does not specifically measure or verify. The 1995 GAO findings of displacement effects also contradict the implied narrative that barriers reduced overall illegal activity. Including the Title 42 context and the lack of systematic barrier-specific measurement would create a significantly different impression than the claim presents.

`cherry-picking`

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification

The claim is partially supportedby [DHS data](https://www.dhs.gov/news/2020/10/29/border-wall-system-deployed-effective-and-disrupting-criminals-and-smugglers) showing significant apprehension decreases in specific barrier sectors (e.g., 87% in FY20 vs. FY19 in areas with new border wall system, 79% in RGV Zone 1, and 60–81% in El Paso zones), but it cherry-picks this data by omitting the critical confounding factor that [Title 42 expulsions](https://www.cbp.gov/newsroom/stats/cbp-enforcement-statistics/title-8-and-title-42-statistics-fy2021) were implemented on March 21, 2020—seven months before the claim—which would independently cause dramatic decreases in border crossings regardless of barriers. The claim also extends to "illegal drug" and "human smuggling" activities, yet the available evidence primarily documents apprehension/crossing decreases, not drug trafficking or human smuggling specifically, and a [1995 GAO report](https://www.gao.gov/assets/ggd-95-30.pdf) found that decreases in barrier sectors were offset by increases in non-barrier sectors due to displacement effects, contradicting the implied narrative of overall reduction. Furthermore, [GAO reported in 2017](https://sgp.fas.org/crs/homesec/R45888.pdf) that CBP had not developed systematic metrics to assess border fencing's contribution to its mission, leaving the claim's specific causal attribution unsubstantiated.